findings.problem:
Native is the default, yet:
- prod's availability depends on a CC credential;
- every org displays a 'sandbox' CC environment;
- any user can trigger a third-party call that reveals vendor configuration;
- the fallback path can transmit fabricated values.

Once native is live, the failure modes that matter are all silent today:
- an MQ disconnect;
- transmissions with no CBP response;
- unmatched inbound messages;
- billing failures;
- a lost rejection email;
- an expiring CBP certificate.
Access tokens also accumulate in container logs.

findings.recommendation:
Add POST /auth/forgot-password:
- always answers 202 (no enumeration);
- is rate-limited per email and per IP;
- issues the existing hashed PasswordSetupToken with a 1h TTL;
- on reset, clears refreshToken, failedAttempts and lockedUntil;
- keeps MFA at the next login;
- sends a security email.
Wire up the link, and give admins a reset action for any user.

findings.problem:
With the ingested USITC rates (DbHtsRateSource), any line that claims one of these returns estimable:false from the estimate and cannot be priced or built for native transmission:
- an NT52 exemption: USMCA-qualifying Canadian or Mexican goods, any 232-covered good via .05.90, civil aircraft, donations;
- the Section 338 exemptions;
- the low-metal 232 heading 9903.82.03.

That covers most Canada/Mexico border trade and every 232 good from the 60 NT52 economies, so native entries for them are blocked.

Users cannot be expected to know CBP's program precedence:
- a wrong order is rejected (F852);
- a duplicate doubles the duty in our estimate;
- a base or ch.98 code entered as an 'overlay' becomes a value-0 tariff with no meaning.

findings.evidence:
- abiDocuments.ts:136-138: "Deliberately NOT an 'HTS 8507.60 from CN needs 9903.88.01' recommender".
- AbiItem (types.ts:96-120) has no declarations or exclusions, and migrateItem (migrateV1.ts:77-87) maps none.
- CERT rules recorded in the scenarios:
  - index.ts:28-31: every line carries its origin country's 9903.05.xx number (F771 otherwise);
  - index.ts:232-236 and 981-983: the NT52 country number is dropped when 9903.05.90 is claimed;
  - 301 China numbers are restricted to CN origin (F603);
  - index.ts:242-250: 232 lines need 54 Type 08/07 (F794 ADDTNL DEC TYPE RQRD) and an extra one draws F773.
- CATAIR July 2026: revision 109 adds 54 Type 12 (copper smelt and cast); ESF-101/103 product exclusions are "in lieu of applicable HTS chapter 99 number"; usage note (ii) bars remedy numbers on Type 51.
- CSMS #68253075: ACE rejects missing copper smelt/cast data from 14 Sep 2026.

findings.problem:
The filer must know from memory:
- the right number for each of 60 NT52 economies and the exemption subdivisions;
- 301 list vs review-action numbers;
- 338 and 232 coverage, and which numbers exclude which.

A missing or extra number is a reject. Every 232 line built through v1 is rejected, because it cannot carry melt-and-pour, smelt-and-cast or copper declarations, or a product exclusion in place of the ch.99 number.

findings.recommendation:
One Field primitive (UFF-16) should:
- always pair a `useId` with htmlFor;
- render hints as visible helper text under the field (the in-bond pattern is the good one) or as a focusable `button` trigger with `aria-describedby`;
- link errors with aria-describedby.

The stepper becomes `<nav aria-label="Filing steps"><ol>` with `aria-current="step"` and sr-only labels at every breakpoint. Icon links get `aria-label`, and row actions use `focus-within:opacity-100 [@media(hover:none)]:opacity-100`. Make the file picker a real button plus a drop zone. Fix the cancelled-pill contrast. Add axe-core to the Playwright suite for these 9 routes.

findings.correction:
All cited infrastructure claims are verified false against the deploy files:
- a single postgres:16-alpine container;
- a 14-day pg_dump with no WAL and no replicas;
- Azure ACS SMTP, not Resend (no Resend code in server/src);
- HTS classify goes through abiGateway.classifyHTS, which is CustomsCity.

The security vendor row already says 'OpenAI (gpt-4o-class models)'.

The same page has more false auth claims that the auditor missed:
- security-client.tsx:194 says 'Per-account rate limits on login attempts. Suspicious activity flagged in the audit log and emailed to admins'. authLimiter (middleware/rateLimiter.ts:13) is keyed per IP, and no suspicious-activity detection or admin email exists in server/src.
- :191 'Sign-up requires a 6-digit code' describes a disabled flow.

The core findings are verified:
- DEADLINE_THRESHOLDS = [72, 48, 24];
- the stale threshold is 72h;
- there is no CSV audit export (only GET /audit-log);
- the only SSO reference is in plans.ts and the chat faq.

Additional inaccuracies:
- bulk-submit is capped at 20 (filings.ts:1219 'Maximum 20 filings per bulk operation'), while the copy says up to 50.
- The bulk path never calls sendDocument for ISF-10 and skips the card gate (see LND-M1), so 'each one shows its status as CBP processes it' is false for ISFs too, not just entries.
- features-client.tsx:391 'HTS Classifier: AI-suggested' is served by the CustomsCity classifyHTS call.

findings.evidence:
`grep -rni sigma landing/app landing/components landing/lib` returns nothing. The terms say "a legally binding agreement between you and MyCargoLens" (terms/page.tsx:~33). The certified entity is Sigma Technology Partners (filer code S7P).

terms/page.tsx:37 describes only "submit Importer Security Filings (ISF) and other CBP filings". terms/page.tsx:84-86 says "We are not a licensed customs broker...". There is no clause covering:
- the client authorising Sigma as its ABI Service Provider (the LOI to their CBP client rep);
- Sigma acting as ISF filer under S7P on the importer's behalf (spec decision 3: per-filing S7P or client code);
- data transmitted under the client's own filer code.

Both legal pages show "Last updated: May 2026" (privacy :22, terms :21).

findings.recommendation:
Before any PROD filing, have counsel add:
- the operating entity ("MyCargoLens is operated by Sigma Technology Partners") in the Terms, Privacy and footer;
- a Service Provider / service-center clause: the client keeps its filer code and its CBP relationship, and authorises Sigma to transmit on its behalf;
- an ISF Filer Agent authorisation for S7P mode, captured per org and per filing in the app (ties into OrgCbpConfig.isfFilerModeDefault);
- updated Last-updated dates.

The Terms clauses are legal work. The landing/ change is just rendering them.

findings.problem:
Native onboarding branches on whether the prospect:
- has its own ABI filer code (and so needs its CBP client rep to add our queue);
- is a licensed broker or a self-filing importer;
- wants Sigma to act as its ISF filer (S7P mode).

The form captures none of this, so the queue-approval lead time starts only after the first call. "Full customs" also implies in-bond, which is not deliverable (LND-03).

